  "worked_solution": "First, let's write the function in a form that is convenient for transformations:\n\n$$\ny=f(1-x)=f(-(x-1))\n$$\n\nWhen performing graph transformations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nSo, to plot the graph of $y=f(-(x-1)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, reflect the graph across the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(-x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268132/assets/0ec555d724ca500d516d4af4e8b8726e88b9213bdc3e0cb6b1c3bec4b04ee861.png)\n\n- Finally, we shift the result horizontally by $1$ unit to the right  and obtain the graph of $y=f(-(x-1)).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268132/assets/32051a03c7bfbcdb28cadfa18320d64035eacc7bf4534760f0ed35ef305aa088.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(-(x-1))$ is $(-4,4].$ \n\n**Note:** Vertical stretches and shifts do not change the domain of the resulting function.",
